BookmarkHub
===========

Self-hosted bookmark manager for families and small teams. One small Docker
container, no database server, no external services, no tracking.

  Image:    ghcr.io/olivermandic/bookmarkhub  (linux/amd64, linux/arm64)
  License:  PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0 - free for personal and other
            noncommercial use, see LICENSE
  German install guide: INSTALL-DE.txt


Features
--------
- Bookmarks with title, description, tags and categories (drag and drop with
  mouse, touch and keyboard); grid, list and compact views; Cmd/Ctrl+K palette
- Several users; every bookmark is private, shared with everyone, or shared
  with chosen people only
- Import from browser bookmark files (Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari) and JSON,
  with preview and duplicate detection; JSON export
- Two-factor sign-in (TOTP, QR code generated locally) with recovery codes
- Automatic daily backups (14 daily + 8 weekly) to a folder of your choice
- Favicons fetched and cached by the server (no Google/DuckDuckGo service),
  weekly dead-link check - both can be switched off by an admin
- Installable web app (PWA) for iPhone, iPad, Android and desktop; light and
  dark mode; 33 languages including right-to-left
- Forgotten admin password: reset from the container shell


Quick start
-----------
  mkdir bookmarkhub && cd bookmarkhub
  curl -O https://raw.githubusercontent.com/olivermandic/bookmarkhub/main/docker-compose.yml
  curl -o .env https://raw.githubusercontent.com/olivermandic/bookmarkhub/main/.env.example
  docker compose up -d
  docker logs bookmarkhub        # shows the random admin password once

Open http://<host>:3131, sign in as "admin" with that password and set your
own. Put it behind your reverse proxy for HTTPS.

Update:
  docker compose pull && docker compose up -d

Data lives in ./data (back it up together with the backup folder).


Forgotten admin password
------------------------
  docker exec -it bookmarkhub reset-admin-password            # asks for a new one
  docker exec -it bookmarkhub reset-admin-password --random   # prints a one-time password
  docker exec -it bookmarkhub reset-admin-password --user NAME
  docker exec -it bookmarkhub reset-admin-password --disable-2fa --keep-password

Without a shell: create an empty file data/RESET_ADMIN_PASSWORD and restart
the container; the one-time password is printed to the container log.


Settings (.env)
---------------
  BOOKMARKHUB_VERSION   latest       image tag, e.g. 2.2.0 to pin a version
  PORT                  3131         port on the host
  TZ                    Europe/Berlin
  BACKUP_PATH           ./backups    folder for the automatic backups
  BACKUP_KEEP_DAILY     14
  BACKUP_KEEP_WEEKLY    8
  COOKIE_SECURE         auto         auto | true | false
  BASE_PATH             /            run under a sub path, e.g. /bookmarks
  FAVICON_FETCH         true         starting value of the admin switch
  LINKCHECK_DISABLED    false        starting value of the admin switch
  UPDATE_CHECK          false        opt-in update notice (see Privacy)


Privacy
-------
The browser only loads files from your own BookmarkHub address: no CDNs, no
web fonts from third parties, no analytics, no cookies except the sign-in
cookie. The server itself makes only these outgoing requests:

- Favicons (default on): the website of the bookmark itself (home page,
  /favicon.ico, web manifest). No cookies or credentials are sent.
- Link check (default on): the bookmark URL, about once a week.
- Update notice (default OFF): when an admin switches it on, the server asks
  https://api.github.com/repos/olivermandic/bookmarkhub/releases/latest at
  most once a day. Only a fixed User-Agent "BookmarkHub-update-check" is sent -
  no user data, no bookmarks, no installation ID. GitHub sees the IP address
  of your server. Admins then see a banner with a link to the release notes.
  Nothing is updated automatically.

Each of the three can be switched off separately under Admin -> External
requests. With favicon fetching off, new bookmarks show a letter avatar;
uploaded icons keep working.


Development
-----------
  npm install && (cd server && npm install)
  npm run build && npm start          # http://localhost:3131
  npm run dev                         # Vite on :8080, proxies /api to :3131
  npm test                            # server tests (Node >= 20)
  tests/ui-test.mjs                   # Playwright UI test (iPhone WebKit, overflow, RTL, GDPR)
  docker compose -f docker-compose.dev.yml up -d --build   # build the image locally

Layout: src/ React UI (Vite, Tailwind), src/i18n/ translations, server/
Express API, docker/ entrypoint and reset tool.

Releases: pushing a tag vX.Y.Z builds and publishes the multi-arch image
(.github/workflows/publish-image.yml). Changes: CHANGELOG.txt.
Security issues: see SECURITY.txt.


